Gals : Lear and implement EPMP

This year, I focused heavily on understanding EPMP end-to-end—not just how the API functions, but how the data flows, what each field represents, and how the preferences ultimately impact our outreach decisions. I took time to study the structure of EPMP responses, how contact information is organized, and how Do Not Contact preferences operate across different categories. This helped me understand, at a deeper level, why certain members should be excluded and how these rules protect both the business and the members.

A major part of the work was building an automated Python script that could process more than 1.1 million MBIs reliably. I designed it to handle token management, retries, batching, and error classification without needing manual fixes.Throughout the project, I validated the accuracy of the responses, checked the data quality, and ensured the output files were clean, consistent, and usable for compliant outreach.

One of the biggest benefits was the ability to identify and remove DNC/DNE members before the outreach lists were generated. This significantly improved the quality of our contact data and reduced compliance risk. By the end of the project, I felt confident in running the EPMP process, troubleshooting issues, and explaining the logic behind the results to others. Going forward, my goal is to continue improving the script and supporting the business as EPMP becomes a core part of our data pipelines.

goal : deliver quality first time, every time 

One of my priorities this year was to make sure that every piece of work I delivered—whether it was a dataset, an analysis, or an API-based output—was accurate from the very first attempt. I focused heavily on validating my pipelines, double-checking the logic, and making sure the numbers made sense before sharing anything with the business. That meant doing my own completeness checks, reconciling counts, reviewing edge cases, and confirming that all business rules were followed correctly.

Over time, this approach reduced the amount of back-and-forth rework and built confidence in the outputs I delivered. For example, when working on large files like the EPMP response data, I made sure every record was accounted for, all errors were categorized, and the business received clean, reliable results they could use immediately. My goal has consistently been to hand off work that doesn’t require revision—something that not only saves time but also strengthens trust in the data.

By taking the extra steps up front and maintaining attention to detail, I ensured that the business received high-quality deliverables the first time, every time. This is a standard I’ve held myself to throughout the year and will continue to maintain in future projects.
